# Explore Information-Theoretic Identity

Begin with behavior: two systems are equivalent when every permitted input history produces the same observation. The interactive parity machine makes this definition tangible.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

## From examples to complete decisions

A finite static profile is equivalent exactly when its set of distinguishing inputs is empty. Edit two profiles and watch that set appear.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{algorithm:0}}

<details><summary>Click to reveal why finite testing fails without structural assumptions</summary>
For any finite set of natural-number probes, choose an untested number and make two Boolean functions differ only there. They pass every selected test but are not equal. See the broader background on [function extensionality](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Extensionality).
</details>

## Infinite histories, finite certificates

Finite machines face infinitely many words, but only finitely many state pairs. Bisimulation records equal observations and closure under every one-step transition.

{{visualization:0}}

{{algorithm:1}}

{{demo:0}}

<details><summary>Click to reveal the bisimulation proof</summary>
A bisimulation implies equal observations after every word by induction on word length. Conversely, relate exactly those state pairs having equal behavior after every future word. The empty word proves equal current observations, and prefixing by one symbol proves transition closure. This is the standard [bisimulation](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bisimulation) fixed-point idea.
</details>

## Finding the experiment that separates two systems

When equivalence fails, breadth-first search on the product graph returns a shortest distinguishing history.

